; DERIVED - not verbatim. RFC 8747 ships no CDDL, no ABNF, no ASN.1 and no
; JSON Schema; see source-renderings.txt for the measurement. This grammar is
; this library's rendering of what section 3, section 3.1 (including Table 1),
; section 3.2, section 3.3 and section 3.4 state in prose, written so that a
; decoder can be tested against it.
;
; WHAT IT IS BUILT FROM, requirement by requirement:
;   rfc-8747#R-0012  the cnf claim value is a CBOR map
;   rfc-8747#R-0028   MUST represent only a single proof-of-possession key
;   rfc-8747#R-0029   at most one of COSE_Key and Encrypted_COSE_Key may be present
;   rfc-8747#R-0025   confirmation members that are not understood MUST be ignored
;   rfc-8747#R-0033     Table 1 row 1: COSE_Key, key 1, value type COSE_Key
;   rfc-8747#R-0034     Table 1 row 2: Encrypted_COSE_Key, key 2, COSE_Encrypt/Encrypt0
;   rfc-8747#R-0035     Table 1 row 3: kid, key 3, binary string
;
; THE INSTANCE THIS GRAMMAR EXISTS TO REJECT, written down so a later pass can
; falsify it cheaply (hazards.md s7 - a schema that parses is not a schema that
; binds):
;
;     { 1: <COSE_Key>, 2: <COSE_Encrypt0> }
;
; A naive rendering misses it. If the extension socket demanded by rfc-8747#R-0025 is
; written as `* label => any` in the SAME map as optional entries for 1 and 2,
; then RFC 8610 s3.5.4 makes the wildcard satisfy every entry the named rules
; reject, and the grammar constrains nothing - which is exactly how rfc-9360's
; derived CDDL parsed for two passes and bound nothing. Two devices prevent it
; here: the cut operator `^ =>` on every named entry, so a wrongly typed value
; under a known label cannot fall through to the wildcard; and `other-label`,
; which EXCLUDES 1, 2 and 3 by construction, so a second key representation
; cannot be absorbed as an unknown member. Both are checked, not assumed - see
; README.md for the accept/reject table.
;
; WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONSTRAIN:
;   - the empty map. Section 3.1 puts the set of members a CWT must contain
;     out of scope ("is context dependent and is outside the scope of this
;     specification"), so {} is not excluded by this document.
;   - co-occurrence of kid with a key representation. Section 3.1 restricts
;     COSE_Key against Encrypted_COSE_Key and says nothing about kid, and
;     section 3.2 explicitly allows a kid INSIDE a COSE_Key. Forbidding
;     { 1: ..., 3: ... } would be this library's rule, not the document's.
;   - COSE_Key, COSE_Encrypt and COSE_Encrypt0 themselves. They are RFC 8152's
;     and this document defines none of them; they are left as sockets.
;   - whether a COSE_Encrypt0 carries its tag. Section 7.2.2 makes the tag
;     optional and section 3.3's example omits it, so BOTH forms are admitted
;     here and a deterministic profile has to choose. See design-notes.md.

cnf-claim = cnf-with-cose-key
          / cnf-with-encrypted-cose-key
          / cnf-without-key-representation

cnf-with-cose-key            = { 1 ^ => COSE_Key,            cnf-rest }
cnf-with-encrypted-cose-key  = { 2 ^ => Encrypted_COSE_Key,  cnf-rest }
cnf-without-key-representation = { cnf-rest }

cnf-rest = (
    ? 3 ^ => bstr,              ; Table 1 row 3: "binary string"
    * other-label ^ => any,     ; the extension socket rfc-8747#R-0025 requires
)

; EXCLUDES 1, 2 and 3, AND NOTHING ELSE. This is what stops the socket
; swallowing a second key representation. CDDL has no negation on literals, so
; the complement is written out — and it must be the WHOLE complement, which
; the first version of this rule was not: it enumerated 0, the negative
; integers, the unsigned integers from 4 up and the text keys, and stopped
; there. CBOR admits ANY data item as a map key, so that rule REJECTED a
; byte-string, boolean, array, map, tagged or bignum key — and rfc-8747#R-0025
; requires a member we do not understand to be IGNORED, i.e. TOLERATED. A
; grammar that rejects it binds more than the document does, which is
; hazards.md s7 in mirror image: not a schema that fails to constrain, but one
; that constrains what the source leaves open. The remaining major types are
; named by major type because that is exhaustive: 2 and 3 as `bstr`/`tstr`, and
; 4, 5, 6 and 7 cover every array, map, tagged item (bignums among them) and
; simple or float value.
other-label = 0
            / nint
            / (4 .. 18446744073709551615)
            / tstr
            / bstr
            / #4 / #5 / #6 / #7

; Sockets. RFC 8747 defines none of these three - section 3.2 and section 3.3
; name them and cite [RFC8152].
COSE_Key           = any
Encrypted_COSE_Key = COSE_Encrypt / COSE_Encrypt0
COSE_Encrypt       = any
COSE_Encrypt0      = any

; The claim as it sits in a CWT Claims Set. Claim Key 8 and value type `map`
; are stated only in section 7.1.1's registration.
;
; KEY 8 IS OPTIONAL, and the `?` was added by the cross-check pass. The first
; version wrote `8 ^ => cnf-claim` with no `?`, which REJECTED `{1: "iss"}` and
; `{}` - a CWT Claims Set that carries no `cnf` at all. Nothing in RFC 8747
; requires one: section 3 is conditional ("By including a "cnf" (confirmation)
; claim in a CWT, the issuer of the CWT declares..."), section 7.1.1 merely
; REGISTERS the claim, and registering a claim does not make it mandatory in
; every CWT. messages.yaml accordingly gives claim key 8 `required: false`, and
; the two now agree. This was `other-label`'s defect one rule later: a grammar
; that binds MORE than the document does. What the rule still binds is the one
; thing it can - the TYPE of key 8's value, by the cut, so
; `{8: {1: ..., 2: ...}}` and `{8: 5}` are still rejected.
cwt-claims-set-fragment = { ? 8 ^ => cnf-claim, * other-claim-label ^ => any }
; EXCLUDES 8 and nothing else, for the reason `other-label` gives above: a CWT
; Claims Set may carry a claim key of any CBOR type, and this document governs
; only key 8.
other-claim-label = 0 / nint / (1 .. 7) / (9 .. 18446744073709551615) / tstr
                  / bstr / #4 / #5 / #6 / #7
